            0.547
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter summary"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.567,
            0.884,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Identities",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are equations that are true for all values of the variables."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.616,
            0.885,
            0.658
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "One of the ways to visualise identities is using geometrical models or algebra tiles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.666,
            0.813,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• Identities can also be used to factor algebraic expressions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.695,
            0.885,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Factorisation of quadratic expressions may be visualised by means of algebra tiles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.745,
            0.885,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• Identities can also be used to simplify calculations such as squaring numbers or evaluating products of numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.795,
            0.886,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rational algebraic expressions may be simplified by factorisation and removing the common factors in the numerator and denominator, provided such a factor exists and it is not equal to zero."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
